---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { frontendSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
/* ── Hero ── */
.fe18-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#00b894 0%,#00cec9 50%,#0984e3 100%)}

/* ── Lint check animation ── */
.lint-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:0.75rem}
.lint-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.75rem;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.78rem}
.lint-code{padding:0.35rem 0.9rem;border-radius:0.5rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.12);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);border:0.0625rem solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2)}
.lint-badge{padding:0.25rem 0.75rem;border-radius:1rem;font-size:0.7rem;font-weight:700;animation:lintFade 0.5s ease both}
.lint-badge.err{background:#ff7675;color:#fff;animation-delay:0.3s}
.lint-badge.ok{background:#55efc4;color:#1a1a2e;animation-delay:0.6s}
@keyframes lintFade{from{opacity:0;transform:scale(0.85)}to{opacity:1;transform:scale(1)}}

.fe18-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}

/* ── Rule cards ── */
.rule-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(15rem,1fr));gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem}
.rule-card{background:#fff;border:0.0625rem solid #e0e0e0;border-radius:0.875rem;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem}
.rule-card-title{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.8rem;font-weight:700;color:#00b894;margin-bottom:0.5rem}
.rule-bad{color:#d63031;font-size:0.82rem;margin:0.25rem 0;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace}
.rule-good{color:#00b894;font-size:0.82rem;margin:0.25rem 0;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace}
.rule-label{font-size:0.72rem;color:#888;display:block;margin-bottom:0.15rem}

/* ── Config section highlight ── */
.config-section{background:#f0faf7;border:0.0625rem solid #b2dfdb;border-radius:0.875rem;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin:1rem 0}
.config-key{color:#00b894;font-weight:700}
.config-val{color:#0984e3}

/* ── Plugin flow ── */
.plugin-flow{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:1.25rem}
.pf-box{padding:0.6rem 1.1rem;border-radius:0.625rem;font-size:0.82rem;font-weight:600;text-align:center}
.pf-vite{background:#f0faf7;color:#00b894;border:0.0625rem solid #b2dfdb}
.pf-arrow{color:#aaa;font-size:1.1rem}
.pf-lint{background:#e3f2fd;color:#0984e3;border:0.0625rem solid #b3d9f7}
.pf-out{background:#fff3e0;color:#e17055;border:0.0625rem solid #ffcc80}

/* ── Quiz theming ep18 ── */
.quiz-ep18f .quiz-q-num{color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep18f .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep18f .quiz-opt-letter{color:#00b894}
.quiz-ep18f .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#00b894;background:#e8f8f5}
.quiz-ep18f .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#e8f8f5;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero fe18-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 19 of 23</div>
  <h1>ESLint &amp; Stylelint —<br>The Code Quality Guards</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">Before your code ever runs, linters read it line by line and flag every mistake — unused variables, wrong equality operators, invalid CSS properties. Like a spell-checker, but for code.</p>
  <div class="lint-art">
    <div class="lint-row">
      <span class="lint-code">if (x == null)</span>
      <span class="lint-badge err">ESLint: use ===</span>
    </div>
    <div class="lint-row">
      <span class="lint-code">color: bleu</span>
      <span class="lint-badge err">Stylelint: unknown value</span>
    </div>
    <div class="lint-row">
      <span class="lint-code">const name = 'Gokul'</span>
      <span class="lint-badge ok">✓ ok</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe18-num">01</span>
  <h2>What Is a Linter?</h2>
  <p>A <span class="highlight">linter</span> is a tool that reads your source code — without running it — and reports problems. Think of it like a very thorough spell-checker that also catches logical mistakes, style inconsistencies, and patterns known to cause bugs.</p>
  <p>There are two linters in the dealmaker-ui repo:</p>
  <ul style="line-height:2;padding-left:1.5rem;margin-top:0.5rem">
    <li><strong>ESLint</strong> — checks JavaScript (and Vue's <code style="font-size:0.875rem">&lt;script&gt;</code> blocks)</li>
    <li><strong>Stylelint</strong> — checks CSS and SCSS (and Vue's <code style="font-size:0.875rem">&lt;style&gt;</code> blocks)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Neither of them runs your code. They <span class="highlight">parse</span> it into a tree, apply rules against that tree, and output a list of violations. The process is: read file → parse → check rules → report.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// ESLint reads this file</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> greeting = <span class="code-string">'hello'</span>   <span class="code-comment">// ← declared but never used</span>
<span class="code-keyword">if</span> (x == <span class="code-keyword">null</span>) { ... }        <span class="code-comment">// ← == instead of ===</span>

<span class="code-comment">// ESLint output (without running the code):</span>
<span class="code-comment">// 1:7  error  'greeting' is assigned a value but never used</span>
<span class="code-comment">// 2:7  error  Expected '===' but found '=='</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe18-num">02</span>
  <h2>What ESLint Catches</h2>
  <p>ESLint enforces rules. Each rule targets one specific pattern. The dealmaker-ui repo uses the <span class="highlight">Airbnb style guide</span> — one of the strictest and most popular rule sets in the JavaScript ecosystem.</p>

  <div class="rule-grid">
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-card-title">== vs ===</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Bad (loose equality):</span>
      <div class="rule-bad">✗  if (x == null)</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Good (strict equality):</span>
      <div class="rule-good">✓  if (x === null)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-card-title">Unused variables</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Bad:</span>
      <div class="rule-bad">✗  const x = 5   // never used</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Good:</span>
      <div class="rule-good">✓  use the variable or remove it</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-card-title">console.log left in</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Bad:</span>
      <div class="rule-bad">✗  console.log('debug')</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Good:</span>
      <div class="rule-good">✓  remove before committing</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-card-title">var instead of const/let</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Bad:</span>
      <div class="rule-bad">✗  var count = 0</div>
      <span class="rule-label">Good:</span>
      <div class="rule-good">✓  const count = 0</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">Beyond Airbnb, the repo also extends <code style="font-size:0.875rem">vuejs-accessibility</code> — rules that catch accessibility mistakes in Vue templates, like missing <code style="font-size:0.875rem">alt</code> text on images or buttons with no readable label.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe18-num">03</span>
  <h2>The .eslintrc.js Config File</h2>
  <p>Every ESLint rule set is configured in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.eslintrc.js</code>. Here is the actual config from the dealmaker-ui repo, broken down piece by piece:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// .eslintrc.js</span>
module.exports = {
  <span class="code-string">root</span>: <span class="code-keyword">true</span>,           <span class="code-comment">// stop ESLint looking in parent folders</span>
  <span class="code-string">env</span>: {
    <span class="code-string">browser</span>: <span class="code-keyword">true</span>,    <span class="code-comment">// window, document etc. are valid globals</span>
    <span class="code-string">es2021</span>: <span class="code-keyword">true</span>,     <span class="code-comment">// modern JS syntax is allowed</span>
  },
  <span class="code-string">extends</span>: [
    <span class="code-string">'airbnb-base'</span>,                <span class="code-comment">// Airbnb JS rules (no React)</span>
    <span class="code-string">'plugin:vuejs-accessibility/recommended'</span>,  <span class="code-comment">// a11y rules</span>
    <span class="code-string">'plugin:vue/recommended'</span>,     <span class="code-comment">// Vue-specific rules</span>
  ],
  <span class="code-string">parserOptions</span>: {
    <span class="code-string">ecmaVersion</span>: <span class="code-string">'latest'</span>,
    <span class="code-string">sourceType</span>: <span class="code-string">'module'</span>,        <span class="code-comment">// ES import/export syntax</span>
  },
  <span class="code-string">rules</span>: {
    <span class="code-string">'object-curly-newline'</span>: <span class="code-string">'off'</span>, <span class="code-comment">// this one Airbnb rule is disabled</span>
  },
  <span class="code-string">ignorePatterns</span>: [<span class="code-string">'dist/'</span>, <span class="code-string">'node_modules/'</span>],  <span class="code-comment">// never lint these</span>
  <span class="code-string">settings</span>: {
    <span class="code-string">'import/resolver'</span>: {
      <span class="code-string">alias</span>: { map: [[<span class="code-string">'@'</span>, <span class="code-string">'./src'</span>]] } <span class="code-comment">// so @ imports resolve</span>
    }
  }
};</pre>
  </div>

  <div class="config-section">
    <p><span class="config-key">extends</span> works like inheritance — you start from a big set of rules and then <span class="config-val">rules:</span> lets you override individual ones. Here, <code style="font-size:0.875rem">object-curly-newline</code> is turned off because the team found it too strict.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe18-num">04</span>
  <h2>Stylelint — The CSS Version</h2>
  <p><span class="highlight">Stylelint</span> does the same job as ESLint but for CSS and SCSS. It catches typos in property names, invalid values, wrong units, and style conventions.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">/* stylelint.config.js */</span>
module.exports = {
  <span class="code-string">extends</span>: [
    <span class="code-string">'stylelint-config-recommended-scss'</span>,     <span class="code-comment">// SCSS rules</span>
    <span class="code-string">'stylelint-config-recommended-vue/scss'</span>,  <span class="code-comment">// Vue &lt;style&gt; blocks</span>
  ],
};</pre>
  </div>

  <p>What Stylelint catches in the repo:</p>
  <ul style="line-height:2;padding-left:1.5rem;margin-top:0.5rem">
    <li><strong>Unknown properties</strong> — <code style="font-size:0.875rem">colour: red</code> (British spelling) is an error</li>
    <li><strong>Invalid values</strong> — <code style="font-size:0.875rem">margin: 5</code> (missing unit) is an error</li>
    <li><strong>SCSS syntax issues</strong> — unclosed mixins, wrong nesting</li>
    <li><strong>Vue-specific</strong> — scoped style problems in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">&lt;style scoped&gt;</code> blocks</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe18-num">05</span>
  <h2>When Do Linters Run? Vite Plugins.</h2>
  <p>Both linters are wired into Vite as <span class="highlight">plugins</span>. In <code style="font-size:0.875rem">vite.config.mjs</code>:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-keyword">import</span> eslint <span class="code-keyword">from</span> <span class="code-string">'vite-plugin-eslint'</span>
<span class="code-keyword">import</span> StylelintPlugin <span class="code-keyword">from</span> <span class="code-string">'vite-plugin-stylelint'</span>

<span class="code-keyword">export default</span> defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    vue(),
    eslint(),           <span class="code-comment">// ← runs ESLint on every .js and .vue file</span>
    StylelintPlugin(),  <span class="code-comment">// ← runs Stylelint on every .vue style block</span>
  ]
})</pre>
  </div>

  <div class="plugin-flow">
    <div class="pf-box pf-vite">You save a file</div>
    <div class="pf-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="pf-box pf-lint">Vite triggers ESLint + Stylelint</div>
    <div class="pf-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="pf-box pf-out">Errors printed in terminal</div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">There are two different outcomes depending on when errors are found:</p>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1rem;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr">
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">During dev (<code>npm run dev</code>)</strong><br>
      <span style="font-size:0.9rem">Lint errors appear in the terminal. The dev server keeps running. You see the error but the app still loads.</span>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">During build (<code>npm run build</code>)</strong><br>
      <span style="font-size:0.9rem">Lint errors <strong>STOP the build</strong>. The dist folder is never created. Nothing gets deployed.</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">Linter</strong> — reads code without running it, flags violations of rules (bad patterns, style issues, bugs).
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">ESLint</strong> — checks JavaScript. Extends Airbnb rules + Vue + accessibility. Config in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.eslintrc.js</code>.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">Stylelint</strong> — checks CSS/SCSS. Extends SCSS and Vue/SCSS rule sets. Config in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">stylelint.config.js</code>.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">When they run</strong> — both are Vite plugins. Dev mode: warnings in terminal. Build: errors block the entire build.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep18f">
  <span class="section-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What does a linter do — does it run your code?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Yes — it runs the code and checks for runtime errors</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>No — it reads and parses the code without running it, then checks rules against the parsed structure</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Yes — it compiles the code and checks if it builds correctly</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>No — it checks the network requests made by the code</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) No — it reads and parses the code without running it, then checks rules against the parsed structure</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">A linter is a static analysis tool — it never executes your code. It parses the source into a tree structure and applies rule checks against that tree. This is why it can catch errors in code that would never be reached at runtime, like an unused variable inside a dead code branch.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What happens if ESLint finds an error during <code>npm run build</code>?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The error is shown as a warning and the build continues</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>The error is written to a log file but the build completes</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>The build stops immediately — no dist folder is created, nothing gets deployed</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>The file with the error is skipped and the rest of the app builds normally</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) The build stops immediately — no dist folder is created, nothing gets deployed</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">During development (npm run dev), lint errors are shown in the terminal but the dev server keeps running — you can still see the app. During a production build (npm run build), lint errors are treated as hard failures that abort the build. This means code with lint errors can never reach production.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What is the difference between ESLint and Stylelint in the dealmaker-ui repo?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>ESLint is for backend code and Stylelint is for frontend code</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>They do the same thing — one is a backup of the other</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>ESLint checks JavaScript (and Vue script blocks); Stylelint checks CSS/SCSS (and Vue style blocks)</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>ESLint runs at build time; Stylelint runs only at deployment</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) ESLint checks JavaScript (and Vue script blocks); Stylelint checks CSS/SCSS (and Vue style blocks)</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">They are separate tools for separate languages. A .vue file has three sections — template, script, and style. ESLint handles the script section (JavaScript), Stylelint handles the style section (SCSS). Both are wired into Vite as plugins, so they both run automatically on every file save during development.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#00b894,#0984e3)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 20?</h2>
  <p>Your app talks to different servers depending on the environment — UAT, staging, production. How does it know which URL to use? The answer is .env files and environment variables.</p>
  <a href="part20.html" class="btn btn-white">Continue to Episode 19 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How the Frontend Works — Part 18: ESLint & Stylelint | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={frontendSeries} episodeNum={19} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
